Dan Morrill wrote:
> 
> 
> How far does it get before it dies?  The first couple times I played with
> it I got a pthread assertion exception, but I've since gotten past that,
> and encountered the infamous floating exception.

Floating point exceptions in programs like Netscape, where you are
practically sure that you do not care as this was result of calculating
some pixel on a border of a display - or something of a similar kind -
are really trivial to deal with.  If compiling with -mieee flag does
not help (exception may happen in libraries which were not compiled
with this flag) then install a custom signal handler which just
returns on SIGFPE.  You will get 0 for a, fake, result but most likely
this was an effect of an underflow so you have a reasonable chance that
this is "right". 

A while ago I posted an example which illustrated various
methods of handling SIGFPE and their effects (it was possibly in
comp.os.linux.alpha).  If necessary I may do that again.

  Michal


