Steve G wrote:
> 
> Ok, here's what I've done.
> 
> Un-tar'ed the egcs-1_0_3a_tar.gz in the /root/egcs dir.
> Did a ./configure
> Did a make
> Did a make install
> 4 hours later did a make bootstrap
> 
> now, two hours later, I'm wondering why when I do a "egcs -version" I still
> get
> 
> [steve@hymen /]$ egcs --version
> egcs-2.90.27 980315 (egcs-1.0.2 release)

If you did not use --prefix=<something> while runing ./configure
then you ended up installing your new compiler as 'gcc' in
/usr/local/bin - I think.  'make install' wrote you a precise
message what it was installing and where.  Try 'gcc -v'.
You will find your brand new compiler IF your have directory in
which you installed a new compiler in your PATH and IF it is there
earlier than a directory with your "other" gcc exectutables.
'egcs' obviously runs Red Hat supplied program which was renamed
to 'egcs' (an extremely bad idea IMO).

Alias 'which' tells you which program you are really trying to run.
Try 'which gcc' and 'which egcs', and 'which cc'.

  Michal


