> Ok...  for the list...
> I did fix my problem.
> Those damn subnet masks got me.
> Actualy it did help me find a misconfiguration of my whole system.
> Can
> someone give me pointer to more on subnet masks.  I don't fully
> understand
> them.  Am I right in saying that all networks defined with a subnet
> mas
> have to be sequential (ie...  192.168.1.0 and 192.168.2.0 not
> 192.168.1.0
> and 192.168.123.0)  do they have to be powers of 2 (ie 1 subenet 2
> subnets 4 subnets 8 subnets?
> 
	Sort of - you're on the right track. The way I find easiest to
think of it
	is in terms of bits. All valid subnet masks AFAIK are all 1's
followed by
	all 0's. The minimum subnet you can have is one with 4 addresses
( 1 for
	the network, 1 for broadcast, 2 for talking on). Everything else
scales up 
	from that (although historically everything has actually scaled
down to it 
	as initially it was just classes A-E and subnetting subnets only
turned up
	relatively recently, although I'm quite possibly wrong about
that).

