The command can be any one that causes MILO to initialize its devices,
e.g. boot, ls, show.  The last couple of lines before the hang are 

scsi0 : ncr53c8xx - revision 3.0i           (i'm using a newer milo this time)
scsi : 1 host.

And I think it's pretty definite that it's the scsi hanging.  The next
step after "scsi : 1 host" is supposed to be a bus scan (and the light
on the cdrom does indeed flash once, suggesting that a bus reset was sent
at least) and a listing of the devices found.

An update: I tried different MILOs, this time booting from ARC.  On the
third try -- all of a sudden -- about 10 seconds after the "scsi : 1 host"
messages, it successfully completed the bus scan and found my disk and
CDROM.  I was then able to list the contents of the disk and -- yes -- even
boot a kernel (avanti didn't work -- used xl from the rh5.1 kernels/ dir)
using the first partition of my disk (it was an already-installed rh5.1 root
fs from my multia) as root.  Worked fine, and next couple of times I booted
there wasn't even a 10 second pause after "scsi : 1 host."  Everything
seemed great.  I figured it probably just needed the right combination of
the ARC and the right MILO...

BUT....

then I put the cover back on the machine and leaned back to enjoy my new
workstation.  On the next boot, once again, ... hang after "scsi : 1 host."
I haven't been able to get it to repeat its performance again.  (And yes,
I tried taking the cover off again :-)  I'm quite certain there aren't any
loose connectors or termination bobbles; been over them a gazillion times.

What do you all think -- do I have a bad box? (I'd hate to have to go to the
hassle of shipping it back to webauction :-(((....)  Or is there a software
solution I'm still missing?

