Hello People,

Not that this list should become a forum, we feel that a reply is required
and in no particular order we have: 

SPAM and Valentin Guillen's email complaint.
--------------------------------------------
LSL is part of an Internet ISP and occasionally are hacked into and mail is
sent by the hacker. Sometimes (as with a recent case) we catch the culprit
in the act and take action. Other times, there is insufficient evidence to
bring legal action. In all instances we find the "hole" and plug it. This
takes time and is  done by the ISP end of the organization. The LSL part
(various email boxes) fills with email varying from folk letting us know we
have a break in (thanks people!) to those hysterically ranting about their
cost of receiving SPAM etc. Those stopping to *think* about it realize that
we have been hacked into, others give a knee jerk reaction and make matters
worse for everyone concerned. As for the Hacker? They just dance off to the
next ISP and do the same again.

So to make things *VERY* clear, LSL does not issue SPAM. We do not condone
SPAM. We apologize for SPAM being sent from our systems. We individually
send email apologies to all those affected and yes, we consider this a
lower priority than finding the security hole and plugging it. There is
only one response we can give - an apology - and this is what we give. The
most recent one is show below:

        -------

    Hi,

    We sincerely apologise for the email you received. LSL is part of an
Internet     company and one of the subscribers (or other unauthorized
individual) appears     to have used our domain to send email.

    Neither LSL or 123.net endorse or support such unauthorized use and we
take     immediate steps against those responsible. 

    Once again, sincere apologies. 

    Warm regards,

        -------

If this is considered minimal, we are not quite sure what else is required.
Full details of the security breach or attack? Details of how it was
plugged? No Way! The Bad Guys would just love that. Or the culprits head,
duly severed on a plate? (Ok, ok, things getting out of hand now).



The CD
------
We didnt want to do this, but as the thread has been started. LSL produced
a full production run of the Redhat 5.0 GPL Alpha CD. Sales were less than
expected (huge understatement) and after the 5.1 update we were left with a
considerable quantity. We expected to manufacture a similar run for 5.1 but
the quality of the released binaries made a full CD production run
uneconomic and we waited till things stabalized. We also left the CD on the
Web to gauge demand.  We dont usually give details of the demand for Linux
products as it can often be counter productive, so lets just say that we
have back-orders for well less than 40 (forty) CD's. We do not have to be
rocket scientists to calculate the loss if we had done a full manufacturing
run. Add to this the cost of testing and we have a _big_ loss (there is
more to CD preparation than just shoving software onto media). About the
time the Fulfillment girls were reassuring the (very few) that the CD would
be released, we became aware of the likely release schedule for RH 5.2  No,
this is not widely known, no we cant tell you what it is, no we dont have a
spy inside Redhat and no we are not going to tell you how we arrived at the
information. 

Now we have a situation where we have to manually produce a CDR, which will
be obsoleted fairly quickly with the resulting howls of outrage.

Why bother? We did it as a SERVICE to the axp Community who have
consistently proven themselves to be highly intelligent, reasonable,
understanding and supportive. Did we do this to make money? ROFL: No Way!!! 


What About My Order!
--------------------
(Someone is bound to ask) Will we continue to produce another RH Axp CD?
Yes, very likely. We supply the Debian version on CDR and will likely
produce the next RH as well. All those who have outstanding orders will be
fulfilled. 


The Final Apology!
------------------
This is beginning to sound like the White House :)  We never entered the
Axp market to make money. We have learned lots about the Axp and the Axp
community (who are very different from the Intel folk) and we got hit by
the lack of demand for the axp CD.  We cant create demand, and we did the
Axp production run as a service to the Linux community. We dont want to
abandon the Axp sector, but we have to pay for the overheads of putting the
CD together and burning them. 

A big sorry to all those that feel let down in some way, but we were trying
to help.

Warm regards,



