The etymology of "twaddle" is interesting, but not apropos.  I think this
is a solecism, and should be "Drive needs twiddling."

From context, I think the message means that your drive has alignment
problems, and needs to be adjusted.  I don't know whether standard PC
floppies will work on these machines, but if so, it's cheaper to buy a new
one.  Alignments are best done with a special disk and oscilloscope, and
aren't worth it for a $12 drive.  I see out-of-line floppy drives in our
lab all the time.  Solution: throw them away!  (CD's also go bad, not just
from dust, but those are more expensive.)

To "twiddle" something is to make fine adjustments to it.  "Twaddle" means
"arrant nonsense".

--Ron Bruck


