Well, I got the 2.1.125 source off of ftp.kernel.org, and with the addition
of the spinlock fix in dma.h, I had no problems compiling it.  Actually, it
threw me a couple of warnings, but nothing that's caused any problems so
far, and it does seem faster than the 2.0.34 kernel I used to have on my
Multia.  I could never get the kernels that came in the
kernel-source-2_0_3X.rpm files to compile, so I figured "What the hell?" and
it ended up working.
  The spinlock fix has been gone over on this list a few times, but here is
what I did to correct it.  Open up dma.c in the kernel subdir of your source
distrib. Add '#include <asm/spinlock.h>'
 after '#include <linux/errno.h>'.  Oh yeah, don't forget to edit the
Makefile and comment out 'SMP=1', the new kernels default to SMP support,
which doesn't work properly on Alphas yet.

