I concur with Wes; you've bought yesterday's workstation and you're
comparing it to today's technology.  The 21064A 233Mhz *was* fast when it
was current.  It is still a fine CPU, but neither it or the mainboard can
be considered "high-end" by today's standards.  All you really need in
order to appreciate it's performance is the right frame of mind; I was
hoping for P100 performance from my AS200 so IMO it's quite fast.  :)

I have compiled some very meaningless benchmarks** on my web page (in the
the sig) that pit the 21064A against several other processors, including
StrongARM, MIPS, Sparc, 21164 and Pents.

** IMHO most "benchmark"-benchmarks are meaningless; we don't buy computers
to run benchmarks all day.  The real benchmark is how well the system
fulfills your needs.  The numbers are there for recreation; not for
decision-making.

