On Wed, 21 Oct 1998, Andrew Hobgood wrote:

> Okay... well.  Another update (darn, I'm busy today. =P):
> 
> I increased my SHM_MAX to 0x420000 as seen in an old post, and that *seems* 
> to have helped matters... but I still have problems with playing audio, 
> especially from a pipe (like cdparanoia | tplay).  The odd, random app
> segfaults seem to have stopped... but...
> 
> When the sounds stuff crashes sometimes, it freezes the box.  Not a total
> freeze, but a kind of freeze that I've never seen anywhere else.  It stops
> all processes from running, but the kernel is still awake.  I can switch
> virtual consoles, I can connect to TCP ports, I can ping the box, that all
> works fine... all the apps are dead, though.  I can't type anything, after
> a TCP port opens up to a service, the service never negotiates, etc.
> 
> Again, I'm using OSS/Linux [commercial] on an AWE64 Gold ISA, kernel 2.0.35
> on a PC164LX 21164a 533MHz with 256MB RAM.

I don't know if you found a solution to this yet, but I've been hacking on
the sound code lately, and it looks like it was written by a monkey.  There
are lots of problems with it.  (ok, so I hope the person that wrote it isn't
on this list!  ;)

In particular, there is a definite memory leak in the mixer code (latest 2.1
kernels). You wouldn't happen to be using modules for your sound drivers,
would you?  The leak shows up easiest if you insmod and rmmod the driver
several times...it'll crash your kernel eventually.

I think the code in the 2.1 kernels was written by the OSS/Linux people, and
probably has the same bugs.  The drivers for OSS/Linux were back-ported to
the 2.0 kernels, (according to the 4front-tech.com pages) so are *probably*
the same... 

I'll post patches to linux-kernel and here when (if...) I get it fixed.

-- Bob

