I've done a couple of AS200's with SRM.  I used fdisk for the initial
install, leaving a few cylinders for the aboot stuff (aboot is not
installed by default).  You don't want to put a filesystem on those
first few cylinders.  The rest of this I may have done the hard way,
but it seems to work....Here's the pertinents:

/dev/sda1 is swap -- ~90M -- starts 3 cylinders from the start of disk
/dev/sda2 is / -- ~1.5G
/dev/sda3 is /opt1 -- ~1G
/dev/sda4 is /opt2 -- whatever was left

These were created with fdisk.  At that point I had a working box, but
was booting from a floppy which was seriously bugging me.

I then found minlabel on the cd which will build and write a bsd label.
He wants everything in terms of size and offset in 512B sectors.  He
also starts counting things at 0 as opposed to fdisk's 1.  I grabbed
a calculator and some paper and crunched the bsd numbers to be equal
to the fdisk table, then had minlabel do its thing.  After that,
I was able to run swriteboot /dev/sda bootlx, etc, etc.  I didn't blow
away the fdisk-created partition table.

One other gotcha I ran into is that minlabel dosen't seem to be able
to view the disk geometry in a translation mode.  I had to tell
fdisk to use the C/H/S that minlabel saw when I did the fdisk table.

I have not played with the b option of fdisk.  Perhaps I could have
saved myself a ton of time.  Matters not.  The boxes work!
-ls-

  



