Jeff DeMaagd wrote:
> 
> Everything seems
> to compile fine, but when I install and reboot, the startup goes normally
> (or so it seems), but at the login prompt, the keyboard does not accept ANY
> input.

Source in drivers/char/keyboard.c includes the following code:

#ifndef __i386__
#define INIT_KBD
static int initialize_kbd(void);
#endif
....
(more of the stuff which is using it).

This 'initialize_kbd()' function is normally off on Intel (and 'on'
on other architectures) because there are around various BIOS/keyboard
hardware combinations which dislike very much if they are poked around.
From time to time there is one which requires this INIT_KBD on (and
usually this runs fine and is even required on non-Intel).  Maybe you
happen to have such setup which dislikes this initialize_kbd() stuff?
Or maybe something is misconfigured and you are not running it?

This is only a guess.

   Michal


