> > used to be!  Four to fifteen times faster (depending on what you're doing) is a
> > great leap forward in performance.
> >
> > Do you have IDE or SCSI?  What kinds of transfer rates do you get?  Here's a
> > quick and dirty test with a 9909415 byte file.
> >
> > With 2.0.34:
> >
> > time dd if=logs/linux-2.1.125.tar.bz2 of=killme.qqq
> > 19354+1 records in
> > 19354+1 records out
> > 0.051u 6.586s 0:10.21 64.9%     0+0k 0+0io 1281pf+0w
> >
> > About 950K/sec wall clock, 1460K/sec CPU time.  And now, for kernel 2.1.125:
> >
> > time dd if=logs/linux-2.1.125.tar.bz2 of=killme.qqq
> > 19354+1 records in
> > 19354+1 records out
> > 0.046u 0.313s 0:02.59 13.5%     0+0k 0+0io 1281pf+0w
> >
> > About 3740K/sec wall clock, 26960K/sec CPU time!!  I'd be curious to know how
> > this compares to SCSI, or fast UW-SCSI, though I imagine it would be
> > drive-dependent...

I guess the "moral of the story" is that with DMA, IDE can be as fast as (or faster
than- depending on the application) `normal' SCSI-II, but fast wide SCSI can be
significantly faster and worth the extra expense for some users.

Thanks for these "data points"!

