>>> Jeff DeMaagd said:
> 
> I've gotten the lockup on three different configs/compiles on 2.0.35.  I
> guess I should revert to 2.0.34, huh?

Are those the pristine 2.0.35 sources? If so, that might be your problem.
The Alpha support in 2.0.34 and 35 was not complete; Alan Cox took some of
my patches but not all into 2.0.34, and I think only compilation problems
were fixed in going to 35.

Please look on gatekeeper.dec.com for:

  /pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/alpha-sources-2.0.35-0.2.tar.gz # prepatched
  /pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/alpha-patches-2.0.35-0.2.gz     # just patches

I've had no problems with keyboard with these patches on any of my test
systems, which include MIATA, PC164, LX164, RUFFIAN/UX, etc.

> Is there any point in making a bug report on the 2.0.35 if at least two of
> us get the same results?

Make one to me if my patches don't fix your problem.

> How do I get a pre-release 36 kernel to see if it has the same problem?

Alan has patches for testing at (IIRC):

	ftp.linux.org.uk/pub/linux/alan/2.0.365pre

> Does anyone know of a good way to keep multiple kernel source trees
> around?  Sometimes I get hit by not re-linking /usr/src/linux to the tree
> I'm trying to compile.  I'd like to have at least one 2.0 and one 2.1
> tree.

I recommend leaving /usr/src/linux *exactly* the way the distribution has
set it up, and doing any kernel compiles in a complete separate place, like
/usr/local/src or wherever.

Under RH, /usr/src/linux is important to be left as-is, as they may have
made kernel header patches to help in the building of apps, which *may* get
broken if you use a different kernel source tree to provide those headers.

> I suppose the worst is untaring a new kernel and forgetting to re-link
> before hand, as sometimes it goes to the ./linux/ directory.

Much safer to put them someplace complete different.

It should be noted, however, that there *are* apps in which it is *very*
important to use the same header files as the kernel which it will be run
from. Xservers are just one example in which this holds true, because of
their dependence on some of the kernel data structures for interprocess
communication, messaging, and shared memory.

--Jay++

